18:29:22.841 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":1483,"version":0,"year":1831,"month":10,"day":13,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Beautiful mild weather. Wind in the morning SW. Changed in the afternoon to SSE. Left Benton directly after breakfast &amp; walked out to Newcastle to meet Stephenson who arrived yesterday, fixed the hope to load Bell &amp; Dixons Walls, but won't get coals for upwards of a fortnight. Took the steamer to Shields.</p>\r\n<p>Found a letter from WBS with the Hopes freight account but no note, brokers commission being changed which WBS thought an error. Paid an average Eligible Club &pound;14.9.3 &amp; gave Stephenson &pound;20 to pay wages. Dined at home at 5 o'clock. At the whist club in the evening &amp; with Bess at night. A rainy &amp; blowing night</p>","pageId":954,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":1429,"lock":null}
18:29:22.842 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":149,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":5,"day":16,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull morning but turned out exceptionally hot. Called upon Messrs Ryan &amp; Parkinson respecting Nuv<sup>n</sup> Fitzmaurice bond who said they were wise endeavouring to raise money to pay off all his debts. To my great surprise I met Cha<sup>r</sup> Hurry who I found had been residing in Dublin for 18 months. Dined at Mackens. Went today to see the Rotunda</p>","pageId":697,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":139,"lock":null}
18:29:22.842 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":149,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":5,"day":16,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull morning but turned out exceptionally hot. Called upon Messrs Ryan &amp; Parkinson respecting Nuv<sup>n</sup> Fitzmaurice bond who said they were wise endeavouring to raise money to pay off all his debts. To my great surprise I met Cha<sup>r</sup> Hurry who I found had been residing in Dublin for 18 months. Dined at Mackens. Went today to see the Rotunda</p>","pageId":697,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":139,"lock":null}
18:29:22.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":190,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":6,"day":26,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>An exceptionally hot day. Attended the Master again for him to look over draft of report, when he thought it would be better to appoint me sole guardian. I cannot make up mind to take in so much trouble difficulties &amp; anxiety that I feel very unhappy. Cannot stand it much longer. Dined at Mackens</p>","pageId":701,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":180,"lock":null}
18:29:22.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":190,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":6,"day":26,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>An exceptionally hot day. Attended the Master again for him to look over draft of report, when he thought it would be better to appoint me sole guardian. I cannot make up mind to take in so much trouble difficulties &amp; anxiety that I feel very unhappy. Cannot stand it much longer. Dined at Mackens</p>","pageId":701,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":180,"lock":null}
18:29:22.994 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2305,"version":5,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":20,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... sails set. Perfectly astonished at the exceeding beauty of the coast, so finely wooded &amp; rich. &lsquo;tis impossible to imagine scenery more splendid.</p>\r\n<p>At &frac12; past 6 o'clock am came to an anchor in Copenhagen roads about &frac34; of a mile from the entrance to the harbour. Dressed ourselves &amp; got on shore about &frac12; past 7 o'clock. The mouth of the harbour is protected by a story battery (an island) called the Crown Battery, on both sides of which vessels can pass in, but on that side next to  <span class=highlight_yellow>Elscaeur</span> is the deepest water. We were obliged to stop at a platform where the <span class=highlight_yellow>Zuarantan</span> Officer is on the right hand side after passing the battery, where after being asked several questions, we were given a pass without which we should not have been allowed to land. The expense of this is one dollar for each person on board. Equal to about 2/3 a <span class=highlight_yellow>p</span><span class=highlight_yellow>r</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ice</span>.</p>\r\n<p>Upon landing, we were <span class=highlight_yellow>a</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ssisted</span> by a person who proved to be <span class=highlight_yellow>Mr</span> Chapman's agent at Copenhagen.  He undertook to do what was necessary for us, such as paying the <span class=highlight_yellow>dices</span> <span class=highlight_yellow>for</span> &amp; sent a person who accompanied us to their counting house. The firm, Heeksher, Peterson &amp; M&ouml;ller from whom we met with every attention.</p>\r\n<p>Called upon Mr Ryan, a personal friend of <span class=highlight_yellow>Mr</span> Chapman, who had given us a letter of introduction to him. From him we also met with much <span class=highlight_yellow>pontificating</span>. At 8 o'clock went to the Hotel D'Angleterre &amp; had our breakfast &amp; afterwards proceeded, accompanied by a clerk of Mr Ryan, a very intelligent young Dame who talked English fluently. Arrived <span class=highlight_yellow>at</span> the town by the ramparts. As our time would not allow us to see much more than the town itself, but with it we were as touched &amp; delighted without exception. Taking it all together, it is the most beautiful city I ever saw. Most of cities have their S<sup>t</sup> Gales Bellengales &amp; but here there does not appear to be anything of the sort. &lsquo;tis all alike most ...</p>","pageId":742,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2245,"lock":null}
18:29:22.994 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2305,"version":5,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":20,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... sails set. Perfectly astonished at the exceeding beauty of the coast, so finely wooded &amp; rich. &lsquo;tis impossible to imagine scenery more splendid.</p>\r\n<p>At &frac12; past 6 o'clock am came to an anchor in Copenhagen roads about &frac34; of a mile from the entrance to the harbour. Dressed ourselves &amp; got on shore about &frac12; past 7 o'clock. The mouth of the harbour is protected by a story battery (an island) called the Crown Battery, on both sides of which vessels can pass in, but on that side next to  <span class=highlight_yellow>Elscaeur</span> is the deepest water. We were obliged to stop at a platform where the <span class=highlight_yellow>Zuarantan</span> Officer is on the right hand side after passing the battery, where after being asked several questions, we were given a pass without which we should not have been allowed to land. The expense of this is one dollar for each person on board. Equal to about 2/3 a <span class=highlight_yellow>p</span><span class=highlight_yellow>r</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ice</span>.</p>\r\n<p>Upon landing, we were <span class=highlight_yellow>a</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ssisted</span> by a person who proved to be <span class=highlight_yellow>Mr</span> Chapman's agent at Copenhagen.  He undertook to do what was necessary for us, such as paying the <span class=highlight_yellow>dices</span> <span class=highlight_yellow>for</span> &amp; sent a person who accompanied us to their counting house. The firm, Heeksher, Peterson &amp; M&ouml;ller from whom we met with every attention.</p>\r\n<p>Called upon Mr Ryan, a personal friend of <span class=highlight_yellow>Mr</span> Chapman, who had given us a letter of introduction to him. From him we also met with much <span class=highlight_yellow>pontificating</span>. At 8 o'clock went to the Hotel D'Angleterre &amp; had our breakfast &amp; afterwards proceeded, accompanied by a clerk of Mr Ryan, a very intelligent young Dame who talked English fluently. Arrived <span class=highlight_yellow>at</span> the town by the ramparts. As our time would not allow us to see much more than the town itself, but with it we were as touched &amp; delighted without exception. Taking it all together, it is the most beautiful city I ever saw. Most of cities have their S<sup>t</sup> Gales Bellengales &amp; but here there does not appear to be anything of the sort. &lsquo;tis all alike most ...</p>","pageId":742,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2245,"lock":null}
18:29:22.996 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2318,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":23,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... humour to go on shore. Obliged to go to bed. The pain &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>opium</span> making me exceptionally drowsy. Bought some flannel &amp; hartshorn in the evening which I put upon my face when I went to bed</p>","pageId":760,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2258,"lock":null}
18:29:22.996 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2318,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":23,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... humour to go on shore. Obliged to go to bed. The pain &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>opium</span> making me exceptionally drowsy. Bought some flannel &amp; hartshorn in the evening which I put upon my face when I went to bed</p>","pageId":760,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2258,"lock":null}
18:29:22.997 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2325,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":9,"day":11,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... since I had the pleasure of seeing him. We afterwards took a Greenwich boat &amp; went down to the Hope upon Dowslands way where we dined off cold mutton. At low water looked at her bottom &amp; gave orders for her to go into the dock to be caulked. Turned out an exceptionally wet evening. Had our tea on board &amp; afterwards took a <span class=highlight_yellow>Mackware</span> stage to Leadenhall Street &amp; then walked to New Inn. Bob Marsh at the theatre.</p>","pageId":773,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2265,"lock":null}
18:29:22.997 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2325,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":9,"day":11,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... since I had the pleasure of seeing him. We afterwards took a Greenwich boat &amp; went down to the Hope upon Dowslands way where we dined off cold mutton. At low water looked at her bottom &amp; gave orders for her to go into the dock to be caulked. Turned out an exceptionally wet evening. Had our tea on board &amp; afterwards took a <span class=highlight_yellow>Mackware</span> stage to Leadenhall Street &amp; then walked to New Inn. Bob Marsh at the theatre.</p>","pageId":773,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2265,"lock":null}
18:29:23.040 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":467,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":3,"day":21,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful spring morning. The Hope got to sea early. Wind NW. Paid Foy boat 13/- Went with Mr &amp; Mrs Tinley to Newcastle. Signed the Hope's cargo bill &pound;176.7.7 The <span class=highlight_yellow>Dmans</span> refused to change less than 21/- to upheld &amp; have I <span class=highlight_yellow>conceded</span> treated me <span class=highlight_yellow>exceptional</span> ill. Dined &amp; stayed all night with D Dixon. Wrote to William.</p>","pageId":728,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":449,"lock":null}
18:29:23.040 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":467,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":3,"day":21,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful spring morning. The Hope got to sea early. Wind NW. Paid Foy boat 13/- Went with Mr &amp; Mrs Tinley to Newcastle. Signed the Hope's cargo bill &pound;176.7.7 The <span class=highlight_yellow>Dmans</span> refused to change less than 21/- to upheld &amp; have I <span class=highlight_yellow>conceded</span> treated me <span class=highlight_yellow>exceptional</span> ill. Dined &amp; stayed all night with D Dixon. Wrote to William.</p>","pageId":728,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":449,"lock":null}
18:29:23.053 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":542,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":3,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... upon one of which, <span class=highlight_yellow>Yelagin</span>. We landed &amp; walked through the plantations, the walk of which are beautiful, in good order &amp; under the management of an English <span class=highlight_yellow>Lumana</span> &amp; and attached to a very fine palace of the Emperor that stands close to the water. Near it is a bridge which we crossed to the opposite Island of <span class=highlight_yellow>Kam</span><span class=highlight_yellow>enny</span>, at the end is a handsome theatre, placed there to be near this summer retreat of the Emperor's. This is a very fashionable theatre &amp; attended principally by the court, but I believe open to all. Here they perform French, Italian &amp; German operas. These islands are the property of the Emperor &amp; noblemen. The grounds most beautifully laid out &amp; always open to the public. The Russians being much more <span class=highlight_yellow>ce</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ri</span><span class=highlight_yellow>bal</span> in this respect than John Bull. They delight in seeing the people strolling about the grounds &amp; have seats placed in all directions for their accommodation. We then proceeded through these fairy grounds to the island of <span class=highlight_yellow>Kres</span><span class=highlight_yellow>tovsky</span> Island where Mr Dye has a cottage for the summer. Here we took tea with his family &amp; late in the evening returned to Petersburg which we did not reach till after 1 o'clock in the morning exceptionally tired with our walk</p>","pageId":750,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":524,"lock":null}
18:29:23.053 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":542,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":3,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... upon one of which, <span class=highlight_yellow>Yelagin</span>. We landed &amp; walked through the plantations, the walk of which are beautiful, in good order &amp; under the management of an English <span class=highlight_yellow>Lumana</span> &amp; and attached to a very fine palace of the Emperor that stands close to the water. Near it is a bridge which we crossed to the opposite Island of <span class=highlight_yellow>Kam</span><span class=highlight_yellow>enny</span>, at the end is a handsome theatre, placed there to be near this summer retreat of the Emperor's. This is a very fashionable theatre &amp; attended principally by the court, but I believe open to all. Here they perform French, Italian &amp; German operas. These islands are the property of the Emperor &amp; noblemen. The grounds most beautifully laid out &amp; always open to the public. The Russians being much more <span class=highlight_yellow>ce</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ri</span><span class=highlight_yellow>bal</span> in this respect than John Bull. They delight in seeing the people strolling about the grounds &amp; have seats placed in all directions for their accommodation. We then proceeded through these fairy grounds to the island of <span class=highlight_yellow>Kres</span><span class=highlight_yellow>tovsky</span> Island where Mr Dye has a cottage for the summer. Here we took tea with his family &amp; late in the evening returned to Petersburg which we did not reach till after 1 o'clock in the morning exceptionally tired with our walk</p>","pageId":750,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":524,"lock":null}
18:29:23.055 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":556,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":13,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>I woke a little after 5 o'clock this morning by the crew preparing to get underway, the wind having changed to the westward, so that our intentions of going on shore at Southend has been frustrated. Turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. The ship not much below Southend there being scarcely enough wind to set us against the tide. A most beautiful morning &amp; exceedingly hot. Passed the Earl Balcarras Indiaman homeward bound. A very fine vessel  &amp; also a 74 foot ship at anchor above the <span class=highlight_yellow>Hove</span> Light. Several smaller vessels principally foreigners. Mr Has Venson <span class=highlight_yellow>proceed &amp;</span> <span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>be</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>d_d</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span>to them. In company with us. A beautiful sight, the sea as smooth as oil. At 10 o'clock am driving with the tide, a dead calm. Fear we shall make but a had tide work. The wind seems much incined to get round to the eastward again. About 10 o'clock a little below the Nou Light, the wind took us all aback, changing to the ESE. After making a few tacks, we increased to a fine breeze &amp; we proceeded down <span class=highlight_yellow>wind</span> gradually increasing our rate to about 7 knots an hour. At &frac14; to 5 &amp; 6 o'clock pm. Passed the <span class=highlight_yellow>Suak</span> Light. We have had a most glorious day, exceptionally hot &amp; singing our <span class=highlight_yellow>phizogs</span> in grand style. Bob &amp; I again <span class=highlight_yellow>dispatched</span> a bottle of Maderia after dinner upon deck. If we go on at this rate, I reckon our stock of wine (originally only &frac12; dozen) will only last three days longer. Today we dined at a little after 12 o'clock sea hours. Employed during the morning in preparing our fishing gear. My cold worse this evening &amp; felt exceedingly chilly. Took a stiff glass of warm rum &amp; water &amp; went to bed. Set steering sails</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":534,"lock":null}
18:29:23.056 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":556,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":13,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>I woke a little after 5 o'clock this morning by the crew preparing to get underway, the wind having changed to the westward, so that our intentions of going on shore at Southend has been frustrated. Turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. The ship not much below Southend there being scarcely enough wind to set us against the tide. A most beautiful morning &amp; exceedingly hot. Passed the Earl Balcarras Indiaman homeward bound. A very fine vessel  &amp; also a 74 foot ship at anchor above the <span class=highlight_yellow>Hove</span> Light. Several smaller vessels principally foreigners. Mr Has Venson <span class=highlight_yellow>proceed &amp;</span> <span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>be</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>d_d</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span>to them. In company with us. A beautiful sight, the sea as smooth as oil. At 10 o'clock am driving with the tide, a dead calm. Fear we shall make but a had tide work. The wind seems much incined to get round to the eastward again. About 10 o'clock a little below the Nou Light, the wind took us all aback, changing to the ESE. After making a few tacks, we increased to a fine breeze &amp; we proceeded down <span class=highlight_yellow>wind</span> gradually increasing our rate to about 7 knots an hour. At &frac14; to 5 &amp; 6 o'clock pm. Passed the <span class=highlight_yellow>Suak</span> Light. We have had a most glorious day, exceptionally hot &amp; singing our <span class=highlight_yellow>phizogs</span> in grand style. Bob &amp; I again <span class=highlight_yellow>dispatched</span> a bottle of Maderia after dinner upon deck. If we go on at this rate, I reckon our stock of wine (originally only &frac12; dozen) will only last three days longer. Today we dined at a little after 12 o'clock sea hours. Employed during the morning in preparing our fishing gear. My cold worse this evening &amp; felt exceedingly chilly. Took a stiff glass of warm rum &amp; water &amp; went to bed. Set steering sails</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":534,"lock":null}
18:29:23.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":582,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":30,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at 8 o'clock. A beautiful morning &amp; exceptionally hot. Found a great many Shields ships laying in the mole amongst them the Gordeson, Cholmley, the Wellington, H Daeghton &amp; &amp; Bob Marsh &amp; I went on board &amp; had our breakfast with H Deyghtow &amp; his wife. The master &amp; J Galbraith went on shore early to Mr Booker to report the ship &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>vansack</span></p>","pageId":746,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":557,"lock":null}
18:29:23.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":582,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":30,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at 8 o'clock. A beautiful morning &amp; exceptionally hot. Found a great many Shields ships laying in the mole amongst them the Gordeson, Cholmley, the Wellington, H Daeghton &amp; &amp; Bob Marsh &amp; I went on board &amp; had our breakfast with H Deyghtow &amp; his wife. The master &amp; J Galbraith went on shore early to Mr Booker to report the ship &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>vansack</span></p>","pageId":746,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":557,"lock":null}
18:29:23.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":629,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":26,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful morning, but exceptionally hot. The wind having got round to the north &amp; an appearance of changing. We after breakfast hauled out of the mole but being quite calm were obliged to get a boat ahead &amp; toe the ship down to the guard ship. Cleared at the guard ship about 12 o'clock &amp; proceeded with a leading wind, but little of it.</p>","pageId":761,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":593,"lock":null}
18:29:23.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":629,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":26,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful morning, but exceptionally hot. The wind having got round to the north &amp; an appearance of changing. We after breakfast hauled out of the mole but being quite calm were obliged to get a boat ahead &amp; toe the ship down to the guard ship. Cleared at the guard ship about 12 o'clock &amp; proceeded with a leading wind, but little of it.</p>","pageId":761,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":593,"lock":null}
18:29:23.082 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":692,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":9,"day":13,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant morning wind. Feeling exceptionally annoyed &amp; hurt by Bob Marsh's conduct, I got up early &amp; left his chambers. Indeed had it not been so late I should have done so last night. Breakfasted with William in Nottingham Place. We afterwards went to <span class=highlight_yellow>vere</span> St Chapel. In the afternoon we walked to Sloan Street &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>we</span> called at Mrs Locks to see Mary &amp; Emma. On our way home had a <span class=highlight_yellow>stroll</span> in Hyde Park. Very few people there. Vincent <span class=highlight_yellow>Fitz</span><span class=highlight_yellow>manian</span> &amp; Bob Marsh dined with us. We intended to have given the latter a hint that his company could be dispensed with, but did not, as it might have caused much unpleasantness. I determined however to tell him what I thought of his conduct, but had not an opportunity. He shall yet hear more about it. A very wet night</p>","pageId":774,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":650,"lock":null}
18:29:23.082 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":692,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":9,"day":13,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant morning wind. Feeling exceptionally annoyed &amp; hurt by Bob Marsh's conduct, I got up early &amp; left his chambers. Indeed had it not been so late I should have done so last night. Breakfasted with William in Nottingham Place. We afterwards went to <span class=highlight_yellow>vere</span> St Chapel. In the afternoon we walked to Sloan Street &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>we</span> called at Mrs Locks to see Mary &amp; Emma. On our way home had a <span class=highlight_yellow>stroll</span> in Hyde Park. Very few people there. Vincent <span class=highlight_yellow>Fitz</span><span class=highlight_yellow>manian</span> &amp; Bob Marsh dined with us. We intended to have given the latter a hint that his company could be dispensed with, but did not, as it might have caused much unpleasantness. I determined however to tell him what I thought of his conduct, but had not an opportunity. He shall yet hear more about it. A very wet night</p>","pageId":774,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":650,"lock":null}
18:29:23.085 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":706,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":9,"day":26,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull morning. Afterwards turned to rain. Wind SW &amp; WSW blowing strong. Engaged all the morning in the office making up the Hope's Petersburg voyage. Dined at home. Mrs S<font color=#BFBFBF>cott</font> put the question to me whether I was the cause of <font color=#0000FF>Ann</font> <font color=#BFBFBF>Maxwell </font>being with child, which on her (Mrs Scott's ) account being in so precarious a state of health, I denied.  Therefor if she hears of it through some kind officious friend &amp; anything happens, be the blame upon their shoulders. I shall not be the cause.</p>\r\n<p>With Ann Stokes this evening</p>\r\n<p>The Childers, Captain <span class=highlight_yellow>Moriay</span> came into harbour</p>","pageId":777,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":664,"lock":null}
18:29:23.085 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":706,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":9,"day":26,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull morning. Afterwards turned to rain. Wind SW &amp; WSW blowing strong. Engaged all the morning in the office making up the Hope's Petersburg voyage. Dined at home. Mrs S<font color=#BFBFBF>cott</font> put the question to me whether I was the cause of <font color=#0000FF>Ann</font> <font color=#BFBFBF>Maxwell </font>being with child, which on her (Mrs Scott's ) account being in so precarious a state of health, I denied.  Therefor if she hears of it through some kind officious friend &amp; anything happens, be the blame upon their shoulders. I shall not be the cause.</p>\r\n<p>With Ann Stokes this evening</p>\r\n<p>The Childers, Captain <span class=highlight_yellow>Moriay</span> came into harbour</p>","pageId":777,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":664,"lock":null}
18:29:23.087 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":718,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":10,"day":8,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A very stormy morning. Exceptional wet &amp; heavy sleet showers. Wind about N. Got breakfast early &amp; took the steam boat to Newcastle &amp; at 10 o'clock got into the Express coach &amp; went to Durham. When we arrived a little after 12 o'clock Richmond had sent his man and gig for me but by some mistake the man supposing I had not come, returned to <span class=highlight_yellow>Burn</span><span class=highlight_yellow>hopeside</span> without me at &frac12; past 12 o'clock. I therefor started &amp; walked out to dinner.</p>","pageId":778,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":676,"lock":null}
18:29:23.088 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":718,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":10,"day":8,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A very stormy morning. Exceptional wet &amp; heavy sleet showers. Wind about N. Got breakfast early &amp; took the steam boat to Newcastle &amp; at 10 o'clock got into the Express coach &amp; went to Durham. When we arrived a little after 12 o'clock Richmond had sent his man and gig for me but by some mistake the man supposing I had not come, returned to <span class=highlight_yellow>Burn</span><span class=highlight_yellow>hopeside</span> without me at &frac12; past 12 o'clock. I therefor started &amp; walked out to dinner.</p>","pageId":778,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":676,"lock":null}
18:29:23.114 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":878,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":12,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Beautiful weather, but very windy at West. Received a letter from ABS in reply to their informing him of Mrs Scott's death, saying he cannot leave London before tonight or tomorrow morning Dined at home. Wes &amp; Fanny Smith excessively angry that we should claim the amount of taxes upon the house paid by us in error &amp; which ought to have been paid by Mrs Scott. Fanny inclined to be very violent, her conduct disgusting &amp; very insulting; displaying more regard for the money than for the aunt. Blew a perfect hurricane with heavy rain the afternoon</p>","pageId":804,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":830,"lock":null}
18:29:23.115 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":878,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":12,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Beautiful weather, but very windy at West. Received a letter from ABS in reply to their informing him of Mrs Scott's death, saying he cannot leave London before tonight or tomorrow morning Dined at home. Wes &amp; Fanny Smith excessively angry that we should claim the amount of taxes upon the house paid by us in error &amp; which ought to have been paid by Mrs Scott. Fanny inclined to be very violent, her conduct disgusting &amp; very insulting; displaying more regard for the money than for the aunt. Blew a perfect hurricane with heavy rain the afternoon</p>","pageId":804,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":830,"lock":null}
18:29:23.131 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":98,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":1,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day but exceptionally cold. Wind from SSE. Dined at home</p>","pageId":693,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":93,"lock":null}
18:29:23.132 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":98,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":4,"day":1,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day but exceptionally cold. Wind from SSE. Dined at home</p>","pageId":693,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":93,"lock":null}
